<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shatapatha Brahmana</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shatapatha Brahmana ('Brahmana of a hundred paths') is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> prose text of the White <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajurveda">Yajurveda</a>, composed in the first half of the 1st millennium BCE and traditionally attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajnavalkya">Yajnavalkya</a>. It explains the Vedic sacrificial rites in detail and contains myths such as Manu's flood and the eastward journey of Videgha Mathava, which records the spread of Vedic culture into Videha.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The text belongs to the Śukla Yajurveda and is often described as the most complete and systematic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanas">Brahmanas</a>, the commentaries on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>. Arthur Berriedale Keith placed it linguistically in the later part of the Brahmana period, the 8th to 6th centuries BCE, and Asko Parpola dates it to around 700 BCE. Scholars have rejected claims of a much earlier date: Witzel noted that it refers clearly to iron and reflects developments later than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atharvaveda">Atharvaveda</a> and only slightly earlier than the time of the Buddha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>It survives in two recensions, the Madhyandina and the Kanva. The 14 books of the Madhyandina fall into two parts: the first nine comment closely, often line by line, on the first 18 books of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajurveda">Yajurveda</a>'s samhita, and the remaining five cover supplementary and newer ritual material. The last book closes with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brihadaranyaka-upanishad">Brihadaranyaka Upanishad</a>. Julius Eggeling, its English translator in five volumes, suggested the name may come from the Madhyandina's exactly one hundred adhyayas, though the Kanva, with one hundred and four, has the same name.</p><p>Its rules for the layering, size and shape of bricks in fire altars are, in the view of Frits Staal, part of the ritual geometry that later produced the Sulba Sutras. A. A. Macdonell noted that, unlike the Samhitas, it expressly calls the Earth circular, comparing it to a wheel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Scholars from Macdonell and Keith to D. A. Soifer hold that several avatars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and their Puranic legends either begin or were greatly developed in this text: Matsya, the fish that guides Manu's ship to a mountain peak, Kurma, Varaha, Narasimha and Vamana, the first five of the Dashavatara. It repeatedly identifies Vishnu with the sacrifice. Soifer sees in its story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a> and the asura Namuchi the prototype of the Narasimha myth. The text also tells of a boar called Emûsha who raised up the earth when it was only the size of a span.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shatapatha Brahmana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Shatapatha Brahmana</b> gives the number of syllables in the Rigveda as 432,000, while the metrical text of van Nooten and Holland (1994) counts 395,563.</blockquote><a href="/work/rigveda">Rigveda · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Shatapatha Brahmana</b> tells how Videgha Mathava, guided by the fire god Agni, carried Vedic culture east across the Sadanira river, probably the Gandak, into the marshy lands of Mithila.</blockquote><a href="/state/videha">Videha</a></li></ul></section>
